    "intro": "Good dwarf city names sound carved out of rock: a hard old name like Thardur Hold, or a plain one that says what the place is, like Lanterndeep or Anvilfire. Dwarf cities tend to have both kinds: a plain name any trader understands and an old dwarvish one. This **dwarf city name generator** gives you both for mountain holds, deep cities, forge cities, ruined holds and trade cities, with a short description to start your D&amp;D campaign or story.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good dwarf city names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator, across its five kinds of city:</p><ul><li><strong>Thardur Hold</strong>: a mountain hold with a short, hard dwarvish name.</li><li><strong>Ramwatch Hold, whose war horn can be heard three valleys away</strong>: a hold with a reputation.</li><li><strong>Lanterndeep</strong>: a deep city named for the light it needs.</li><li><strong>Haldgar Deep, where the miners sing to the stone before they cut it</strong>: a description that's also a custom.</li><li><strong>Anvilfire</strong>: a forge city, plain and loud.</li><li><strong>Rustgate, abandoned so quickly the tables are still laid</strong>: a ruined hold with a mystery attached.</li><li><strong>Fallen Ormrak, whose gate was broken by a dragon</strong>: a ruin that explains itself.</li><li><strong>Dralorn Gate, where outsiders may trade but never stay the night</strong>: a trade city with one strict rule.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Holds, deeps and delves",
        "content": "<p>Dwarf cities in fiction owe a lot to Tolkien's Khazad-dûm, the great dwarf realm the elves called Moria, \"black chasm\", and which in English was the Dwarrowdelf. <em>Delve</em> comes from the Old English word for to dig, which is why it turns up in so many dwarf names. The Norse myths that gave Tolkien his dwarves also gave them a home: Niðavellir, where the dwarves forged the gods' treasures.</p><p>The generator has five kinds of city:</p><ul><li><strong>Mountain holds</strong> are fortresses in the peaks: <em>Eaglecrown</em>, <em>Ulmgol Hold</em>.</li><li><strong>Deep cities</strong> are far under the mountain: <em>Borthak Below</em>, <em>Gemhall</em>.</li><li><strong>Forge cities</strong> are named for their fires and anvils: <em>The Forges of Korgol</em>.</li><li><strong>Ruined holds</strong> are Fallen, Old or Lost: <em>Gloomdelve</em>, <em>Old Bramheim</em>.</li><li><strong>Trade cities</strong> are gates and crossings: <em>Goldcross</em>, <em>Galdzan Crossing</em>.</li></ul><p>Choose <strong>With a description</strong> for a line of history or local colour with each name.</p>"
      },
